</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Details about textual attributes in <span id="S3.F3.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MMTAD</span> dataset:<span id="S3.F3.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (Left) Examples showing a subset of all possible combinations of textual attributes i.e. <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}\times T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}\times T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, refer section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Architecture ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. (Right) Chart showing the distribution of annotated attributes in our dataset.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview</span>: Given a document image as input, our objective is to predict the corresponding textual attributes for each word - see Fig.<a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.1 Stage-1: Data Selection Pipeline ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. In the first stage, we obtain word bounding boxes using a text detector<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. The output is processed through a novel data selection pipeline that generates context windows. Our method ensures that the collection of context windows for each document image includes all the words in the document and each context window contains equal number of words. In the second stage, the context windows are fed into the proposed model as input to obtain per-word text attribute predictions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Stage-1: Data Selection Pipeline</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Determining textual attributes of an isolated word solely on the basis of its appearance is a challenging task. A word that appears bold in isolation may not exhibit the same attribute when analyzed in the context of the entire document (see Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>-E). Similarly, a word that seems underlined individually could actually be part of a table structure (Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>-C). This highlights the need to incorporate the neighborhood context to accurately infer textual attributes. Most documents contain approximately 300-500 words, making it impractical to use all word bounding boxes as contextual information. Additionally, the self-attention mechanism in transformers exhibits quadratic complexity concerning input tokens, which significantly increases computational costs during both training and inference, especially for the documents with large word counts. Therefore, we propose a novel efficient data selection pipeline that extracts the relevant local context for the TAR model, allowing it to effectively capture and utilize the neighborhood’s textual style information for each word (see Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Stage-1: Data Selection Pipeline ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times w\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times w\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> represent a document image, where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> denote the height and width of the document image respectively. A Word Bounding Box (WBB) is defined as a rectangular region enclosing a word. To incorporate contextual information, we define a Context Window (CW) around an anchor word in <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> . The anchor word refers to the selected word for which the CW is determined, while all other words within this window are considered as candidate words. This CW encloses the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S-1</annotation></semantics></math> nearest WBBs, determined using the weighted Chebyshev distance metric, defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{Chebyshev}}(c,a)=\max(k\cdot|c_{x}-a_{x}|,m\cdot|c_{y}-a_{y}|)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>Chebyshev</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{Chebyshev}}(c,a)=\max(k\cdot|c_{x}-a_{x}|,m\cdot|c_{y}-a_{y}|)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the candidate WBB center is denoted by <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and anchor WBB center is denoted by <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> are constant weights to get a rectangular context region with <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\neq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\neq m</annotation></semantics></math>.
The context window is designed to be rectangular (with width &gt; height) to align with the document’s horizontal flow of text lines.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13151v1/data-selection-pipeline.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/227;" width="476" height="227" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">The proposed data selection pipeline:<span id="S4.F4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (a) Selection of anchor point. (b) Using the distance metric to get the nearest WBBs to the anchor point forming a <span id="S4.F4.9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CW</span>. Here, the <span id="S4.F4.9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">iso-surface</span> is a set of WBBs equidistant from the anchor using the distance metric. Note that <span id="S4.F4.9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CW</span> is a iso-surface containing <math id="S4.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> words. (c) Forming a sequence input of words in <span id="S4.F4.9.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CW</span> for TAR model.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Each CW comprises <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> word images, all resized to fixed dimensions while maintaining a predetermined aspect ratio.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The number of CWs per document image vary with the number of words <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> in the CW and the density of words in the document.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Naively generating a CW for every word would result in 300-500 context windows per document which is a substantial storage overhead. To tackle this challenge, we propose the concept of Sequential Context Windows for a given document image <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. This approach adopts a strategy similar to Breadth-First Search, where each Word Bounding Box (WBB) is gradually included in a CW through an iterative selection process. Initially, we mark all the WBBs in <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> as ‘unvisited’. In each iteration, an anchor word is randomly selected from the unvisited WBBs in <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. We construct a new CW using the <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S-1</annotation></semantics></math> nearest neighbours from the anchor word. All WBBs within this CW are marked as ‘visited’. This ensures that they are not selected as anchor words in subsequent iterations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This process repeats iteratively until all WBBs in the document are marked as ‘visited’. Our approach ensures a controlled overlap between context windows, striking a balance between storage efficiency and contextual coverage.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13151v1/model.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/243;" width="476" height="243" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the method:<span id="S4.F5.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (a) Describes the extraction of context window <math id="S4.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CW" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CW</annotation></semantics></math> from the document. (b) Architecture of TexTAR, which takes in the sequence of word images (<math id="S4.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CW" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CW</annotation></semantics></math>) and outputs the text attributes for each word in <math id="S4.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CW" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CW</annotation></semantics></math>. (c) It decribes our postprocessing module i.e. <span id="S4.F5.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CAvg</span> for both <math id="S4.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Architecture</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe the architecture of our proposed model, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR</span>, for Textual Attribute Recognition. We leverage the data selection pipeline (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Stage-1: Data Selection Pipeline ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) to extract the context windows of word bounding boxes from the document image. Given a context window <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CW" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CW</annotation></semantics></math> as input, the objective is to predict the textual attributes for all the words in this particular <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CW" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CW</annotation></semantics></math>. More formally, let the Context Window <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CW" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CW</annotation></semantics></math> be defined as
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CW=\{w_{1},w_{2},\dots,w_{S}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CW=\{w_{1},w_{2},\dots,w_{S}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sequence size of the context window. <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR</span> maps each word in the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CW" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CW</annotation></semantics></math> to a pair of textual attributes <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> :</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{TexTAR}(w_{i})\to(T_{1},T_{2})\quad\forall w_{i}\in CW" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">TexTAR</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{TexTAR}(w_{i})\to(T_{1},T_{2})\quad\forall w_{i}\in CW</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\in\{\texttt{normal, bold, italic, bold \&amp; italic}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">normal, bold, italic, bold &amp; italic</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in\{\texttt{normal, bold, italic, bold \&amp; italic}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\in\{\texttt{normal, underline, strikeout, underline \&amp; strikeout}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">normal, underline, strikeout, underline &amp; strikeout</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in\{\texttt{normal, underline, strikeout, underline \&amp; strikeout}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we describe the component modules of <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature Extractor Network <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(FEN)</span></span>:
We pass each word to a CNN network. The output from the CNN’s fully connected layers provide a rich basic representation which is further refined by subsequent modules.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CW" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CW</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a set of <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> feature embeddings, denoted as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="CW_{emb}=\{{FEN}(w_{i}):i=1,\dots,S\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>F</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CW_{emb}=\{{FEN}(w_{i}):i=1,\dots,S\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transformer Encoder Blocks (<span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TEnc</span>)</span>: The embeddings obtained from the FEN are passed to the Transformer Encoder. This encoder allows the model to learn the broader context of the window which is crucial for disambiguating challenging cases.
Essentially, the encoder helps the model infer the correct attributes by attending to the surrounding context and learning the overall pattern of the context window (e.g. presence of a table, underline pattern that extends across all the words of a text line.(see Fig <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>-C)). This contextual understanding ensures that the model does not rely solely on local visual features extracted from FEN but also considers the global structure and relationships within the sequence via the Transformer attention mechanism.
Notably, Absolute Positional Embeddings (APE)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> are <span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">not</span> added to <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CW_{emb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CW_{emb}</annotation></semantics></math>, as they were found to reduce the performance of certain attributes (refer Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)</p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T_{emb}=\{{TEnc}({CW_{emb}^{i}})):i=1,\dots,S\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{emb}=\{{TEnc}({CW_{emb}^{i}})):i=1,\dots,S\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attention blocks with RoPE-Mixed (<span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoPE-MixAB</span>)</span>: Typically, the positional embeddings, such as sinusoidal or learnable embeddings, are added to the <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QKV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QKV</annotation></semantics></math> representations either at the first layer or to all the transformer layers. However, in our task, we found that this approach degrades the overall performance (Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>). To address this, we integrate <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoPE-Mixed</span> positional embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> to form self-attention blocks with RoPE-Mixed PEs. We refer to these blocks as <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoPE-MixAB</span>.
<span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoPE-Mixed</span> embedding is well suited to our use case since it effectively captures diagonal frequency patterns and demonstrates strong extrapolation capabilities. This enables the model to maintain positional awareness of the WBBs surrounding a given word within a continuous 2D space (i.e. normalized 2D coordinates of WBBs in a document image) and enhances localization capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To balance both contextual understanding and positional encoding, we first obtain the sequence of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{emb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{emb}</annotation></semantics></math> from the Transformer Encoder blocks (<span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TEnc</span>) and then we apply <span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoPE-MixAB</span> to incorporate spatial positional information into <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{emb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{emb}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T_{rope}=\{\texttt{RoPE-MixAB}(T_{emb}^{(i)}):i=1,\dots,S\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">RoPE-MixAB</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{rope}=\{\texttt{RoPE-MixAB}(T_{emb}^{(i)}):i=1,\dots,S\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">After processing through the RoPE Attention Blocks, the enriched embeddings are concatenated with the original outputs of <span id="S4.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TEnc</span>.</p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T_{final}=\{T_{emb}^{(i)}\mathbin{\copyright}T_{rope}^{(i)}:i=1,\dots,S\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">©</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{final}=\{T_{emb}^{(i)}\mathbin{\copyright}T_{rope}^{(i)}:i=1,\dots,S\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbin{\copyright}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>©</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbin{\copyright}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes per-token concatenation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dual classification heads</span>: The concatenated embeddings are then passed into two separate feed-forward network (FFN) heads, each designed for a specific attribute classification task group, viz. <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Building on the discussion in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 MMTAD Dataset ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we employ a dual head approach to classify predictions either based on their effect on text (<math id="S4.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) or their prominent visual characteristics independent of text (<math id="S4.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>). This strategy is empirically better than a single classification head (Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CAvg module (<span id="S4.SS2.p12.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CAvg</span>):</span> The class logits generated by FFNs are processed within this module. For each word, the logits obtained from multiple overlapping CW predictions are averaged (see Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.1 Stage-1: Data Selection Pipeline ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>-(c)). Finally, the index corresponding to the maximum logit value determines the class label of the output attribute. This postprocessing step is carried out for both <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> i.e. <span id="S4.SS2.p12.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CAvg (<math id="S4.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.p12.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CAvg (<math id="S4.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments and Comparisons</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted extensive experiments on our MMTAD dataset. The dataset is divided into 1005 train, 137 validation, and 481 test document images. From these images, we extract context windows to form the input data for our <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR</span> model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
