<td id="S5.T2.3.1.12.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="S5.T2.3.1.12.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">0.87</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.1.12.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="S5.T2.3.1.12.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">0.99</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.1.12.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="S5.T2.3.1.12.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">0.99</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T2.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.T2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Ablative studies for TexTAR:<span id="S5.T2.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>) Based on the notations in table, TexTAR is equivalent to <span id="S5.T2.11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR-base-RoPE-Mix-DH</span>. Note that <span id="S5.T2.11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">u &amp; s</span> is the short form for <span id="S5.T2.11.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">underline &amp; strikeout</span> attribute and <span id="S5.T2.11.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">b &amp; i</span> is the short form for <span id="S5.T2.11.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bold &amp; italic</span> attribute. All the above results are reported using <math id="S5.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> metric.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we assess the impact of the Dual Classification Heads, the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoPE-MixAB</span> module and Concatenation procedure (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\copyright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>©</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\copyright</annotation></semantics></math>) in our model architecture. We conduct experiments on the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MMTAD</span> test dataset. Our analysis focuses on an important subset of textual attributes, including <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bold</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">italic</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">underline</span>, and <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bold &amp; italic</span>. Our evaluation metric is the <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> score.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We establish a <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR-base</span> model, using <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FEN</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TEnc</span> and a single classification head having number of output nodes equal to all possible combinations of attributes (<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}\times T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}\times T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>). This architecture serves as the foundation for our ablations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of Dual Classification Heads :</span> We analyze the impact of Dual Classification Heads in our architecture. A performance decline is observed in <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR-base</span> (Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>-I), primarily due to the skewed distribution of cross-combinations in the <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> groups (<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}\times T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}\times T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>), such as <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">italic</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">underline</span> or <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bold</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">strikeout</span>, within the training dataset. To address this imbalance in <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}\times T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}\times T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> combinations, we introduce two separate classification heads for the <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> groups. This design enables multi-task learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, improving overall performance by effectively increasing the number of samples per attribute while restricting gradient flow within each group. By preventing feature distortions in their independent heads, the Dual classification head (<span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DH</span>) approach i.e. <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR-base-DH</span> (Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>-I) achieves superior performance compared to a single-head counterpart. Fig. <a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> presents ablation studies on <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR-base-DH</span>, showcasing its optimal performance before incorporating positional information in subsequent experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of RoPE-MixAB module :</span> We analyze the impact of positional embeddings on the predictions both qualitatively and quantitatively (see Fig <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). For ablative studies, we consider various positional embeddings like APE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, Learnable PE (LPE), and RoPE-Mix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> for 2D-setting. The <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoPE-MixAB</span> module consists of Attention Blocks (<span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AB</span>) with RoPE-Mix PE. Here, <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AB</span> consists of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> self-attention-based transformer encoder layers, where each layer can incorporate positional embeddings (PE) into the attention module. We conduct experimentations based on two training strategies : (1) End to End training of whole architecture (E2E) and (2) Pretrain, then Finetune. In the E2E strategy, our training pipeline components include <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FEN</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AB</span> (<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>=6) with particular PE and <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DH</span>. We perform experiments on <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AB</span> with different PEs like APE, LPE and RoPE-Mixed (Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>-II). Though <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR-RoPE-Mix-DH</span> dominates over others, but the <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">underline</span> performance significantly drops (0.87 to 0.83) and <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">italic</span> performance also decreases slightly (0.94 to 0.93). Further, to preserve the pre-trained features of <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR-base-DH</span>, we adopt the strategy of fine-tuning the <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AB</span> with PE variant keeping the training pipeline as <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FEN</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TEnc</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AB</span> (<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>=2) with particular PE and <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DH</span>. The pretrained weights for <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FEN</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TEnc</span> are loaded and frozen, while we fine-tune the <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AB</span> with PE variant and <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DH</span>. We observe that the different PE variants i.e. <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.22" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR-base-APE-DH</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR-base-LPE-DH</span> (Table- <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>-III) exhibit a good performance over the E2E strategy. But, <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.24" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR</span> (i.e. a RoPE-Mix variant) outperforms both qualitatively and quantitatively (see Fig <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). In Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>-IV, <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR (E2E)</span>, trained E2E on the same architecture as TexTAR, shows performance drop. This justifies the dominance of pretraining and then fine-tuning strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of Concatenation (©) :</span> We conduct an experiment on TexTAR by removing the concatenation layer, referred to as <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR w/o ©</span> (Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>-IV). As observed in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>-IV, the performance of <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">italic</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bold &amp; italic</span> drops, which may be due to the dominant positional bias in embeddings from <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoPE-MixAB</span> (<math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{(i)}_{rope}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{(i)}_{rope}</annotation></semantics></math>). It leads to more dependence on positional information and neglecting learned attribute-specific features. To balance this, we concatenated the output from pre-trained <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TEnc</span> (<math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{(i)}_{emb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{(i)}_{emb}</annotation></semantics></math>) and embeddings from <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RoPE-MixAB</span> (<math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{(i)}_{rope}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{(i)}_{rope}</annotation></semantics></math>), i.e. <span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR</span> which seem to outperform the other variants as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of Augmentation : </span> We observe that removing augmentations leads to a consistent drop in performance across <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> &amp; <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> attributes: bold drops from 0.92 to 0.87, italic from 0.95 to 0.93, and underline from 0.87 to 0.81. This highlights the importance of augmentation for robust style classification. Our method remains detector-agnostic and operates on bounding boxes produced by any text detector. To assess sensitivity to detector choice, we additionally evaluated Hi-SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.The improvements of TexTAR over other ablative studies are demonstrated through qualitative results in Fig. <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13151v1/ablations.png" id="S5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/354;" width="476" height="354" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F8.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="S5.F8.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative examples for ablation studies:<span id="S5.F8.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Examining the qualitative improvements for <span id="S5.F8.11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TexTAR</span> over its ablation experiments. The <span id="S5.F8.11.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red dotted circles</span> highlight errors made by other ablations, while <span id="S5.F8.11.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#404040;">dark gray dotted circles</span> indicate errors by ours, relative to the ground truth. Note that the numbering <span id="S5.F8.11.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">i-(j)</span> (below ablation name) denotes the <span id="S5.F8.11.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">j</span>th row of <span id="S5.F8.11.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">i</span>th section in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments and Comparisons ‣ TexTAR : Textual Attribute Recognition in Multi-domain and Multi-lingual Document Images" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TexTAR is a multi-task, context-aware Transformer for textual attribute recognition (TAR) that leverages self-attention and 2D RoPE positional embeddings (RoPE-MixAB) to classify attributes such as bold, italic, underline, and strikeout at the word level. We introduce MMTAD, a fully annotated multilingual, multidomain dataset for TAR, and demonstrate that TexTAR accurately captures subtle typographic distinctions by encoding fixed-length Context Windows (CWs) around each word—extracted via an efficient detector  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> producing per-window logits which our CAvg module then averages across overlapping CWs. Operating as a post-detection pipeline, our method avoids the irregularities of joint detection–classification, offers computational efficiency through localized CW chunking, and effectively incorporates local positional information via 2D RoPE. Extensive evaluation on MMTAD confirms TexTAR’s font- and language-agnostic robustness and state-of-the-art performance in nuanced text attribute prediction. By combining context awareness, spatial encoding, and post-window aggregation, TexTAR advances both accuracy and efficiency over existing approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
